NAME
    clusterkey - this machine's cluster identity

SYNOPSIS
    clusterkey
    clusterkey new [-f]
    clusterkey peers
    clusterkey line <name> <ip>
    clusterkey realm [new [-f]]
    clusterkey user <name>
    clusterkey held

DESCRIPTION
    Shows, generates and inspects the Ed25519 identity a machine uses to
    authenticate itself to the rest of the cluster. The files live in
    /etc/cluster: id (this machine's private key, mode 0600), id.pub, and
    authorized (the peers this machine accepts).

    With no arguments, prints this machine's public key.

    new generates a fresh keypair. It REFUSES if the machine has no entropy
    device, rather than falling back to something weaker: a guessable machine
    key is worse than none, because it looks like security. On QEMU that means
    booting with -device virtio-rng-device; on hardware without one, generate
    the key elsewhere and copy /etc/cluster/id in.

    It also refuses to replace an existing identity unless given -f. Replacing
    it makes every peer's authorized file stale and this machine unreachable
    until they are all updated.

    peers lists the authorized peers, and reports any line it cannot read - a
    mistyped key is a peer that is silently unauthorized in a file that still
    looks correct.

    line prints the authorized line another machine should hold for this one,
    ready to paste into its /etc/cluster/authorized. It never adds the root
    flag: whether that machine trusts this one with root is its own decision,
    made by appending " root" to the line there.

    realm prints the cluster's realm, /etc/cluster/realm: one word, the same
    on every node of a cluster and unique to it, which every user's cluster
    key is derived with. realm new generates one from the entropy device,
    refusing without one, and refuses to replace an existing realm unless
    given -f, since a new realm changes every user's key. Copy the file to the
    other nodes. A node with no realm derives no user keys, and logs in as it
    always did.

    A user's key in the realm is registered at an export by a line in its
    /etc/cluster/users (0600): "<name> <public key>". An export serves a
    registered user only with a request signed by that key, which a machine
    can make only while the user is logged in on it, with one exception: while
    the export has a `cpu` run in flight to a machine as that user, it serves
    that machine's claims of the user (the run's /host reads) until the run
    ends. An unregistered user is served on the machine's word. A new realm
    changes every key, so every line.

    user <name> prints that line for a password typed twice (echo off), on
    standard output alone, so `clusterkey user ann >> /etc/cluster/users`
    writes just the line. Anyone may run it: the user types their own
    password and hands the line to whoever keeps the exports' registries,
    which registers an existing account, or remakes every line after a new
    realm, without resetting a password. The password is not checked against
    /etc/shadow; a mistyped one makes a line the export refuses the user by,
    until it is replaced. useradd and passwd print the line too, for the
    password they set.

    held (root only) lists the users whose cluster keys netd holds right now:
    login derives a user's key and hands it over, netd signs that user's
    requests with it, and logout drops it. It names uids, never keys.

EXIT STATUS
    0   success
    1   no identity, no entropy, or an unreadable authorized line
    2   bad usage

SEE ALSO
    useradd, passwd, login - where a user's key is derived
    id - the calling task's user identity, a different thing entirely
